# Code: app and lib

Paths: `app/` (playbook name `application`), `lib/` (playbook name `libs`), template `templates/kotlin-microservice/` (playbook name `java-21-microservice`).

| Topic | SSOT |
| ----- | ---- |
| The contract and the algorithm of a service | `documentation/specifications/` (structure: `playbook/workflow/specifications-structure.md`) |
| Specification before code, contract before implementation | `playbook/principles/spec-first.md`, `playbook/principles/contract-first.md` |
| Twelve-factor requirements | `playbook/principles/twelve-factor.md` |
| Names and notation | `playbook/principles/naming-and-notation.md` |
| Kafka consumer and producer from generated code | `documentation/specifications/messaging/README.md` (sections "Kafka consumer в сервисе", "Kafka producer в сервисе") |
| Platform decisions for REST, database, Kafka, codegen, calls between services | rule `adr-topics` |
| Hierarchy of groups in `app/` and `lib/` | `playbook/workflow/repository-topology.md` (sections `app`, `lib`) |
| Steps for a new service | `playbook/workflow/change-flow.md` (section "Новый микросервис или frontend-приложение") |
| Code review and automatic gates | `playbook/workflow/generic-git-flow.md`, `playbook/workflow/delivery-flow.md` |
| Done criteria | `playbook/workflow/dod.md` (sections "Код", "Автоматические ворота", "Согласованность с верхними ступенями") |

Before you write code, do these checks:

1. Find the specification of the service. If it does not exist, stop and tell the user.
2. If the code and the specification do not agree, tell the user. Do not change the specification to agree with the code.
3. Put database migrations in `data-schemas/`, not in `app/`.
4. Write the contract of a library in the README of the library. Do not put it in `documentation/specifications/`.
5. Put configuration that is different between environments in the environment, not in code.
